/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (In many healthcare centers, child vaccination records are still maintained manually through paper-based registers or basic spreadsheets. This traditional approach presents several challenges:)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [17]
>>
endobj
359 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (1. Human Errors: Calculating vaccination due dates manually often leads to mistakes, increasing the risk of missed or delayed immunizations.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [18]
>>
endobj
360 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (2. Data Loss: Paper records are vulnerable to damage, misplacement, or loss, making long-term tracking unreliable.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [15]
>>
endobj
361 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (3. Lack of Reminders: Parents are not notified about upcoming or overdue vaccinations, which results in lower vaccination coverage.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
362 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (4. Inefficient Reporting: Generating reports for audits, follow-ups, or healthcare planning is time-consuming and inaccurate.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [13]
>>
endobj
363 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (5. Limited Accessibility: Parents and healthcare workers cannot easily access or share vaccination histories when needed.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [16]
>>
